          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. The tiny brown or gray bugs most often found in bathrooms are springtails or booklice, which feed on mold and moisture and do not bite or damage the house. They are a sign the room stays damp. Improve ventilation, run the exhaust fan longer after showers, fix leaks, and the population generally declines."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the tiny brown bugs that jump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Indoors, it is almost always springtails or fleas. Springtails cluster in damp spots such as bathrooms, basements and around plants, and do not bite. Fleas are found where pets sleep, and you may notice them on your ankles. If you have pets and the bugs are biting, treat it as fleas and start with your veterinarian."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do tiny brown beetles bite?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Pantry beetles, carpet beetles and foreign grain beetles do not bite people. Some people do get an itchy rash from contact with carpet beetle larvae's bristly hairs, which extension entomologists note can be mistaken for bites. Bed bugs, fleas and ticks are the small brown insects that genuinely feed on people or pets."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a dehumidifier get rid of booklice?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It addresses the cause. Booklice feed on mold growing on damp paper and other starchy materials, and Penn State Extension recommends keeping relative humidity below 50 percent to prevent both. A dehumidifier holding a basement at about 50 percent, combined with removing damp cardboard and mildewed paper, is the standard fix. Spraying is rarely needed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does it take to get rid of pantry beetles?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on how thoroughly you find every infested item. Adults already loose in the house can keep turning up after the source is gone, which is why UC IPM suggests keeping susceptible foods sealed for several months and checking a pheromone trap weekly. Catches that continue mean an infested item is still somewhere."
          }
        },
