        <div id="identify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Small Brown Bug Is It? Start With the ID Table
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before anything else, catch one. Press a strip of clear packing tape over it, or tip it into a zip-top bag, and look at it under a magnifying glass or your phone camera zoomed all the way in. Most of the decisions in this guide come down to three details you can only see up close: whether it has a hard shell or a soft body, whether it has wings or wing covers, and what its antennae and waist look like. A sharp photo is also what a pest professional or your county extension office will ask for if you need a second opinion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then answer four questions. Where did you find it: pantry, bathroom, basement, bedroom, windowsill? Does it crawl, fly, or jump when disturbed? Is it roughly the size of a sesame seed, smaller than a pinhead, or closer to a grain of rice? And are you seeing one now and then, or dozens in the same spot every day? Location and behavior narrow the field faster than color does, because a lot of very different insects are simply small and brown.
          </p>

