                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1/2 in as adults</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Light brown, two dark stripes behind the head; nymphs smaller and darker</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Kitchens, bathrooms, behind appliances</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A breeding population nearby</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small brown ants</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1/16 to 1/8 in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pinched waist, elbowed antennae</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Trails to food or water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A nest in a wall or just outside</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sizes in the table come from university extension fact sheets, chiefly UC IPM, Penn State Extension and the University of Minnesota Extension, and the bed bug description follows the EPA. Treat them as typical adult sizes; immature insects are smaller, and a single specimen can sit at either end of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most useful habit when reading the table is to let location overrule appearance. A small brown beetle on a kitchen windowsill is almost always a pantry beetle that hatched in a package and flew toward the light. A beetle that looks nearly identical, found in a brand-new house in August, is far more likely to be a foreign grain beetle feeding on mold inside damp walls. The same tiny pale insect in a box of old paperbacks in the basement is a booklouse. Same color, same size, three completely different fixes.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the bug does not match anything here, or you suspect bed bugs and want certainty before spending money, take the tape sample to your county extension office. Many land-grant universities run an insect identification service through those offices, often free or for a modest fee, and an exact name is worth more than any product on the shelf.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pantry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Pantry Beetles and Weevils: Find the Package, Not the Bug
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pantry beetles are the most common answer to small brown bugs in a kitchen. Drugstore beetles, cigarette beetles, sawtoothed grain beetles, flour beetles and weevils all live their entire lives inside dry food, and the adults wander out to walls, counters and windows. UC IPM notes that the source is usually a package that was damaged at the store, or an opened package that was used once and forgotten at the back of a shelf. That is the whole problem: one package, quietly producing beetles for months.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is also why sprays fail here. UC IPM's guidance is blunt: insecticides are not recommended for pantry pests, and spraying on or near food may do more harm to your household than the beetles would. Removing the infested food and cleaning thoroughly is the control, even for large infestations. The job is detective work, not chemistry.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">One forgotten package is usually the entire infestation.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pantry beetles do not nest in walls or come in from outside in meaningful numbers. Find every infested item and the population collapses on its own. Miss one and it starts again.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The pantry clean-out, step by step</h3>

          <p>
            Step one: empty the shelves completely and inspect every dry item, not just flour and cereal. The places people miss are spices (cigarette beetles are notorious for paprika and chili powder), dry pet food and treats, birdseed, dried flowers and potpourri, decorative corn or gourds, dried pasta in thin boxes, and anything in the garage or basement storage. UC IPM also flags rodent nests and rodent bait stations as breeding spots. Look for live insects, tiny round exit holes in packaging, webbing in corners, and fine powder that is not the product itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step two: bag anything infested and take it straight outside to the trash. Step three: vacuum the empty shelves, paying attention to shelf-pin holes, the gap at the back where the shelf meets the wall, and under any shelf liner, because spilled flour and the beetles feeding on it collect there. A crevice tool on any vacuum works; a <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> is handy if the shelves are in a garage or basement. Wipe down with soap and water afterward. You do not need to spray the cabinet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step four: decide what to do with items you are unsure about. University of Florida extension guidance is to hold suspect food in a freezer at 0°F for four days, or heat it in an oven at 130°F for 30 minutes, to kill any hidden stages. Freezer air is not the same as the temperature at the center of a dense bag of rice, so give it the full time, seal the item in plastic to limit condensation, and use it soon after it thaws. Freezing kills the insects but does not remove them, so heavily infested food is better discarded.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step five: put everything back into glass jars or rigid plastic containers with tight-sealing lids. Paper, cardboard and thin plastic bags are not barriers; several of these beetles chew straight through them. UC IPM suggests keeping susceptible foods sealed or refrigerated for at least several months after the clean-out, and using a pheromone trap labeled for pantry pests to catch stragglers. Check the trap weekly, and if it keeps catching beetles, there is still an infested item somewhere, so go back through the shelves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="damp" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Booklice, Springtails and Foreign Grain Beetles: The Humidity Bugs
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the group people most often misdiagnose, and the one where a can of spray is least useful. Booklice, springtails and foreign grain beetles do not eat your food and do not bite. They eat mold, fungi, algae and decaying organic matter. Seeing them in numbers is less a pest problem than a moisture reading: something in that room is staying damp long enough to grow the microscopic food they live on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Booklice, also called psocids, are soft, wingless and only 1 to 2 millimeters long, usually pale to dark tan. Penn State Extension explains that indoors they feed on mold and fungi growing on starchy materials such as paper, book bindings, wallpaper, cardboard and stored grain. They show up in basement storage boxes, on damp windowsills, in bathrooms and in new houses that are still drying out. Penn State's recommendation is to keep relative humidity below 50 percent, which prevents both the mold and the booklice, and it states plainly that pesticides are not normally necessary to control booklice in homes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Springtails are similar in size and spring into the air when disturbed, thanks to a tail-like structure folded under the body. University extension services describe them clustering in bathrooms, kitchens, crawlspaces and basements, around sinks, floor drains and overwatered houseplants. UC IPM's advice is to air out and dry infested areas and repair leaks; Ohio State Extension adds that total-release aerosols, the so-called bug bombs, are not recommended because they rarely reach the cracks where springtails hide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foreign grain beetles are the curveball. Despite the name they are not really pantry pests; the University of Minnesota Extension describes them as tiny reddish-brown beetles found especially in new construction in late summer, feeding on fungi growing on moist lumber and drywall inside wall voids. They tend to collect near windows and lights. Populations usually fade as the building dries, and South Dakota State Extension notes that reducing indoor humidity helps them fade faster.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHERE HUMIDITY GENUINELY MATTERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">For these three, drying the room is the treatment.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The EPA recommends keeping indoor humidity below 60 percent, ideally between 30 and 50 percent. Penn State's target for booklice is under 50. Get there and the mold film these insects eat stops growing, and the population declines without any pesticide.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            So start by measuring. A basic digital hygrometer costs little and tells you whether the room is actually humid or you are guessing; our <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer guide</a> covers which ones read accurately. If a basement or storage room sits above 60 percent for weeks at a time, a properly sized dehumidifier is the fix that addresses the cause, and our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> covers which units hold that line through a humid summer. Our guide on <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a> explains why 50 percent is the setpoint to use. If you would rather start without buying anything, <a href="/how-to-keep-a-basement-dry-without-a-dehumidifier/">these steps to keep a basement dry without a dehumidifier</a> handle the drainage and ventilation side first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then remove the habitat. Get cardboard boxes off the basement floor and into lidded plastic bins, pull stored items a few inches away from exterior walls so air can move behind them, and throw out paper or books that are visibly mildewed. If you find actual mold growth on walls or stored belongings, deal with it directly; our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/">getting rid of mold in a basement</a> walks through it. Once the food source is gone and the air is drier, the bugs follow.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="carpet-beetles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Carpet Beetles: The Bristly Brown Larvae Under the Furniture
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the small brown thing looks like a tiny, fuzzy, banded caterpillar, or you keep finding papery brown shells along baseboards and in closets, you are looking at carpet beetle larvae or their shed skins. The adults are small, rounded beetles, often mottled with white, brown and yellowish scales, and they are commonly seen on windowsills in spring. UC IPM describes the varied carpet beetle adult at about 1/10 inch and the black carpet beetle at 1/8 to 3/16 inch, with black carpet beetle larvae reaching about 5/16 inch.
          </p>

          <p>
            The adults are not the problem; they feed on pollen and often arrive on cut flowers or through open windows. The larvae are the ones that do damage. UC IPM lists their food as wool, silk, leather, fur, feathers, natural-bristle brushes and pet hair, with occasional spices and grains. They do not eat synthetic fibers. Damage tends to be spread over an area rather than scattered holes, and unlike clothes moths they do not spin webbing. An adult on the windowsill is a sign that larvae are developing somewhere in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is finding what they are eating. Check the edges of wool rugs and the floor under them, closets holding wool coats or stored blankets, upholstered furniture, pet beds and the lines of pet hair that build up along baseboards and behind furniture. Also check places that collect dead insects, such as light fixtures, window tracks and heating vents, and the attic if birds or wasps have nested there. Larvae feed on all of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then clean thoroughly. Vacuum floors, baseboards, closet corners, under furniture and inside heating returns, and empty the canister or bag outdoors right away. Wash washable items in hot water or have them dry-cleaned. For items that cannot be washed, UC IPM gives two non-chemical options: heat the item to 120°F or higher for at least 30 minutes if it can tolerate it, or seal it in a plastic bag and freeze it below 18°F for two weeks. Store clean woolens in airtight containers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Insecticides are a minor part of this. UC IPM notes they may not be necessary if food sources are removed, and when they are used it should only be as a spot treatment on sites listed on the label, never on clothing or bedding. One more thing worth knowing: some people develop an itchy rash from contact with the larvae's bristly hairs, and extension entomologists note it is sometimes mistaken for bed bug bites. If you have unexplained bites but find only shed skins, carpet beetles are worth ruling out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="biters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Bed Bugs, Fleas and Ticks: The Three That Need a Plan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most small brown bugs are nuisances. Bed bugs, fleas and ticks feed on blood, and they are the ones where getting the identification right and responding methodically matters most. The EPA describes adult bed bugs as reddish-brown, flat, oval, wingless and about the size of an apple seed. They cannot jump or fly. After feeding they swell and look more elongated and redder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect before you treat. Look along mattress seams and piping, under the box spring dust cover, in the joints of the bed frame and headboard, and in the nightstand nearest the bed. Signs include live bugs, rusty or reddish spots on sheets and the mattress, dark specks of droppings, tiny pale eggs and shed skins. Several harmless insects, including spider beetles and carpet beetles, get mistaken for bed bugs, so a confirmed specimen is worth having before you spend money on treatment.
          </p>

          <p>
            If they are bed bugs, the EPA's do-it-yourself guidance includes running bedding and clothing through a clothes dryer on high heat; holding items that cannot be heated in a sealed bag in a freezer at 0°F for three days, using a thermometer because home freezers are not always accurate; and using sealed mattress and box spring encasements so bugs inside are trapped. The EPA warns that foggers will not reach the cracks where bed bugs hide and should not be your only method, and that any pesticide should be an EPA-registered product with bed bugs listed on the label, used according to its directions. It also cautions that DIY heat treatments might not work. Realistically, an established infestation usually ends with a professional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fleas are smaller, flattened side to side, and jump. You usually notice them on your ankles or around wherever the dog or cat sleeps. The fix runs through the pet: talk to your veterinarian about a flea control product, wash pet bedding, and vacuum carpets, upholstery and pet resting areas thoroughly, emptying the vacuum outdoors. Treating the house while the pet keeps carrying fleas in does not work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ticks found indoors are most often the brown dog tick, which the CDC notes can complete its entire life cycle indoors, unlike most ticks. They tend to turn up crawling on walls and curtains near where dogs rest. Again, start with the vet and a tick preventive for the dog, then clean the resting areas and seal cracks along baseboards. One critical safety point: never use a dog flea or tick product on a cat. Some dog products contain permethrin, which product labels warn is toxic to cats.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="roaches-ants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cockroach Nymphs and Small Brown Ants: Bait, Don't Spray
          </h2>

          <p>
            A small brown insect running for cover when you switch on the kitchen light at night is worth a closer look, because young German cockroaches are small and brown. UC IPM describes adults at about half an inch, light brown, with two dark stripes on the shield behind the head. They prefer kitchens and bathrooms, favor warm, humid spots near food and water, and can squeeze into a crack 1/16 inch wide. Seeing nymphs means there is a breeding population close by, not a stray that wandered in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sanitation comes first because it makes everything else work. Store food in sealed containers, vacuum crumbs from cracks and behind the stove and refrigerator, fix dripping faucets and leaks under sinks, and get rid of cardboard and paper clutter they hide in. Then use baits. UC IPM states that baits provide better control than sprays, that sprays do not provide long-term control and can scatter cockroaches to other areas, and that foggers are generally discouraged. Baits are slower; UC IPM notes it may take seven days or longer before you see fewer cockroaches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small brown ants are usually easier to tell apart because they travel in trails. Odorous house ants are about 1/8 inch, dark brown to shiny black, and give off a strong smell when crushed. Pharaoh ants are tiny, about 1/16 inch, and more yellow or orange than brown. Both nest in wall voids, around water pipes and behind baseboards, and both forage toward sweets, grease and moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            UC IPM calls baits the key tool for managing ants and the only type of insecticide recommended in most situations. Place them along active trails, do not spray while baits are in use, offer small amounts of more than one bait type to see which the ants prefer, and replace them when empty. UC IPM notes results can take 5 to 10 days. The trail disappearing slowly is a sign it is working; the ants are carrying bait back to the nest.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever product you use, read and follow the label, which is a legal requirement under federal pesticide law, not a suggestion. Use enclosed bait stations or place gel baits where children and pets cannot reach them, store products in their original containers, and keep sprays off surfaces where food is prepared. If ants with wings are appearing indoors, that is a different question, and our guide to <a href="/small-black-flying-bugs-in-house/">small black flying bugs in the house</a> covers how to tell flying ants from termite swarmers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When to Call a Pro, and What Prevention Actually Looks Like
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most small brown bugs in a house are a DIY job, because the fix is cleaning, storage or humidity rather than pesticide. A professional is worth calling in a handful of specific situations: bed bugs that survive a careful round of DIY treatment, German cockroaches showing up in more than one room, ticks that keep reappearing after the pet has been treated, pantry beetles that return after a thorough clean-out (which can point to a hidden source such as a rodent nest or spilled grain in a wall void), and anything you suspect might be termites or carpenter ants.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you do call, have specimens ready on tape or in a sealed bag, and expect the company to identify the insect before treating. Ask what they identified, what product they plan to use and where, and whether the plan includes inspection and follow-up rather than a single spray. A good company working in an integrated pest management approach will talk about sanitation, sealing and moisture as well as products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Equally important is knowing when not to call. Booklice, springtails and foreign grain beetles respond to drying and cleaning, and a perimeter spray does nothing about the humidity or mold that feeds them. Pantry beetles respond to a clean-out. Paying for recurring treatments for either is paying to manage a symptom you could remove.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Prevention Habit</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why It Works</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Bugs It Prevents</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Store dry food in glass or rigid plastic with tight lids</td>
