                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bed bugs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Look down that table and the pattern is clear: food storage, moisture and cleaning cover almost everything. None of it is expensive and none of it involves spraying the house. The one place spending real money tends to pay off is moisture, because a basement that stays at 65 or 70 percent humidity all summer will keep growing the mold that booklice and springtails need no matter how often you clean. That is the single most common reason these bugs come back year after year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying before you know what it is</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A general-purpose spray kills the few insects you see and does nothing about an infested bag of flour, a damp box of books or a pet carrying fleas. For pantry pests UC IPM does not recommend insecticides at all. Identify the bug first; the right fix is often free.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Throwing out the obvious package and stopping there</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pantry beetles rarely stay in one item. The flour gets tossed while the paprika, the dog treats and the birdseed in the garage keep producing beetles. Check every dry good in the house, including decorations and pet food, or the problem returns within weeks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting off a bug bomb</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Total-release foggers coat open surfaces but do not reach the cracks where bed bugs, springtails and cockroaches hide, and they can scatter insects into new areas. The EPA warns foggers should not be your only bed bug method, and UC IPM generally discourages them for cockroaches.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating humidity bugs with pesticide</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Booklice, springtails and foreign grain beetles are living on mold. Spraying them without drying the room leaves the food source in place, so a new population replaces the one you killed. Penn State notes pesticides are not normally necessary for booklice; humidity control is the fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every brown bug near the bed is a bed bug</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carpet beetles and spider beetles are commonly mistaken for bed bugs, and a misdiagnosis can mean an expensive treatment you did not need. The reverse mistake, dismissing a real bed bug, is worse. Capture a specimen and confirm it before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do I have tiny brown bugs in my kitchen but no open food?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pantry beetles live inside sealed-looking packages, spices, pet food and dried decorations, and the adults fly toward windows and lights, so you see them far from the source. Check every dry item including spices and pet treats. If the bugs are soft and pale rather than hard-shelled, they may be booklice responding to damp, not food.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are small brown bugs in the bathroom dangerous?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. The tiny brown or gray bugs most often found in bathrooms are springtails or booklice, which feed on mold and moisture and do not bite or damage the house. They are a sign the room stays damp. Improve ventilation, run the exhaust fan longer after showers, fix leaks, and the population generally declines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the tiny brown bugs that jump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Indoors, it is almost always springtails or fleas. Springtails cluster in damp spots such as bathrooms, basements and around plants, and do not bite. Fleas are found where pets sleep, and you may notice them on your ankles. If you have pets and the bugs are biting, treat it as fleas and start with your veterinarian.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do tiny brown beetles bite?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pantry beetles, carpet beetles and foreign grain beetles do not bite people. Some people do get an itchy rash from contact with carpet beetle larvae's bristly hairs, which extension entomologists note can be mistaken for bites. Bed bugs, fleas and ticks are the small brown insects that genuinely feed on people or pets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier get rid of booklice?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It addresses the cause. Booklice feed on mold growing on damp paper and other starchy materials, and Penn State Extension recommends keeping relative humidity below 50 percent to prevent both. A dehumidifier holding a basement at about 50 percent, combined with removing damp cardboard and mildewed paper, is the standard fix. Spraying is rarely needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to get rid of pantry beetles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how thoroughly you find every infested item. Adults already loose in the house can keep turning up after the source is gone, which is why UC IPM suggests keeping susceptible foods sealed for several months and checking a pheromone trap weekly. Catches that continue mean an infested item is still somewhere.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are tiny brown bugs in my bed always bed bugs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Carpet beetle larvae, spider beetles and even booklice can turn up in bedding. Bed bugs are flat, oval, wingless and about the size of an apple seed, and they leave rusty spots and dark specks along mattress seams. Capture one on clear tape and compare it against the EPA description, or have your county extension office identify it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

